1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,263
A developer's push is blocked. A push sends
your commits to the server, and this one

2
00:00:09,303 --> 00:00:13,781
was refused. Good: the protection worked.
The developer does the natural thing.

3
00:00:13,821 --> 00:00:17,097
They delete the file, commit
the deletion, and push again.

4
00:00:17,437 --> 00:00:21,161
Blocked again, with the same
message, naming the same commit.

5
00:00:21,501 --> 00:00:25,369
Now they're confused and a little
annoyed, because the file is gone.

6
00:00:25,409 --> 00:00:30,197
Some developers at this point reach for the
bypass, choose a reason from a list, and

7
00:00:30,237 --> 00:00:32,496
the secret goes to the server after all.

8
00:00:32,836 --> 00:00:37,060
You know from the last video why the
second push was blocked: a deletion adds a

9
00:00:37,100 --> 00:00:41,270
commit and removes nothing, and the push
would still deliver the first commit.

10
00:00:41,310 --> 00:00:45,804
What this developer didn't know is that they
were standing at the one moment where a

11
00:00:45,844 --> 00:00:50,122
full repair is cheap. In this video you
make that repair, and you learn what the

12
00:00:50,162 --> 00:00:54,278
block covers and what it doesn't. Keep
those words in mind: the cheap moment.

13
00:00:56,118 --> 00:00:59,701
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

14
00:00:59,741 --> 00:01:04,453
The last video ended on a hard fact: after
the first push, you can't un-publish.

15
00:01:04,493 --> 00:01:08,551
So the valuable controls are the
ones that act before the first push.

16
00:01:08,591 --> 00:01:12,887
This video is about those, and about
the rest of a repository's baseline.

17
00:01:13,227 --> 00:01:18,285
Two words first. A secret here is a
credential, such as an API key or a token:

18
00:01:18,325 --> 00:01:23,775
something that proves to a service who is
asking. And a commit is one saved snapshot

19
00:01:23,815 --> 00:01:27,893
of the project, so a push delivers
snapshots, not single files.

20
00:01:28,233 --> 00:01:33,772
Two sections. Section 21B.12: secret
scanning and push protection on GitHub, what

21
00:01:33,812 --> 00:01:39,558
they cover, what they don't, and a local
analogue of a push-time check in plain Git.

22
00:01:39,598 --> 00:01:44,793
Section 21B.13: Dependabot, code scanning,
and a way for outsiders to report

23
00:01:44,833 --> 00:01:46,378
vulnerabilities to you.

24
00:01:46,718 --> 00:01:51,498
Keep the layers apart with care in this
video. Everything about secret scanning and

25
00:01:51,538 --> 00:01:55,621
push protection is platform behavior,
described from the documentation.

26
00:01:55,661 --> 00:02:00,325
Nothing was run against GitHub. The
demonstration is a pre-receive hook on a bare

27
00:02:00,365 --> 00:02:05,145
repository that you control. A hook is a
file that Git runs at a defined point of a

28
00:02:05,185 --> 00:02:09,791
command. It reproduces the behavior of a
push-time check so that you can see it.

29
00:02:09,831 --> 00:02:12,579
It is not how GitHub
implements push protection.

30
00:02:14,419 --> 00:02:15,997
Here's what you'll walk away with.

31
00:02:16,337 --> 00:02:17,749
After this video you can:

32
00:02:18,089 --> 00:02:23,292
say what secret scanning and push protection
do and name kinds of leak they do not

33
00:02:23,332 --> 00:02:23,611
stop;

34
00:02:23,951 --> 00:02:28,788
model a push-time check locally and show
why deleting the file does not unblock the

35
00:02:28,828 --> 00:02:29,081
push;

36
00:02:29,421 --> 00:02:33,322
remove a secret from unpushed commits
so that the push is accepted;

37
00:02:33,662 --> 00:02:37,726
write a Dependabot configuration for
a Python project, Docker and Actions;

38
00:02:38,066 --> 00:02:41,322
say what code scanning and private
vulnerability reporting add.

39
00:02:43,162 --> 00:02:48,193
Secret scanning scans the entire Git
history on all branches, plus issues, pull

40
00:02:48,233 --> 00:02:52,879
requests, discussions and wikis. It
runs for free on public repositories.

41
00:02:52,919 --> 00:02:58,206
Organization-owned private and internal
repositories need GitHub Secret Protection,

42
00:02:58,246 --> 00:03:03,148
which costs 19 dollars per active committer
per month and is sold on Team and

43
00:03:03,188 --> 00:03:04,239
Enterprise plans.

44
00:03:04,579 --> 00:03:09,411
Push protection blocks a push that contains
a recognized secret before it reaches

45
00:03:09,451 --> 00:03:11,696
the repository. It comes in two forms.

46
00:03:12,036 --> 00:03:17,621
Push protection for users: on by default
since the twenty-ninth of February 2024.

47
00:03:17,661 --> 00:03:22,760
It requires nothing, on GitHub.com
only. It protects your pushes to public

48
00:03:22,800 --> 00:03:28,593
repositories. And a bypass leaves no alert,
unless repository protection is also on.

49
00:03:28,933 --> 00:03:33,712
Push protection for repositories: off
by default. It requires GitHub Secret

50
00:03:33,752 --> 00:03:38,852
Protection, which is free on public
repositories. It protects every push to that

51
00:03:38,892 --> 00:03:42,193
repository, by anyone. And
a bypass leaves an alert.

52
00:03:42,533 --> 00:03:48,078
It covers command-line pushes, commits made
in the web interface, file uploads, REST

53
00:03:48,118 --> 00:03:53,264
API requests, and, on public repositories,
interactions through the GitHub MCP

54
00:03:53,304 --> 00:03:53,729
server.

55
00:03:54,069 --> 00:03:58,563
By default anyone with write access can
bypass a block by choosing a reason.

56
00:03:58,603 --> 00:04:02,799
"It's used in tests" and "It's a false
positive" create a closed alert.

57
00:04:02,839 --> 00:04:07,750
"I'll fix it later" creates an open one.
The bypass is written to the audit log and

58
00:04:07,790 --> 00:04:12,165
emailed to administrators. Delegated
bypass, part of Secret Protection for

59
00:04:12,205 --> 00:04:16,460
organization-owned repositories,
restricts who may bypass and puts other

60
00:04:16,500 --> 00:04:20,636
contributors' requests through a
review that expires after seven days.

61
00:04:20,976 --> 00:04:25,816
What it does not cover. The textbook says
GitHub's default protection is narrower

62
00:04:25,856 --> 00:04:28,226
than most engineers assume. Four points.

63
00:04:28,566 --> 00:04:33,164
Quick quiz first. Push protection
for users is on by default.

64
00:04:33,204 --> 00:04:37,422
Does it check your push to a
private repository? A, yes.

65
00:04:37,462 --> 00:04:38,866
B, no. Your answer?

66
00:04:43,706 --> 00:04:44,102
B.

67
00:04:44,442 --> 00:04:48,343
User push protection guards only
pushes to public repositories.

68
00:04:48,383 --> 00:04:53,160
A private repository without Secret
Protection has no push-time check at all.

69
00:04:53,200 --> 00:04:55,286
Almost everyone assumes otherwise.

70
00:04:55,626 --> 00:05:00,139
It blocks high-confidence provider
patterns, the known shapes of particular

71
00:05:00,179 --> 00:05:04,994
providers' keys. Generic secrets such as
passwords and connection strings aren't

72
00:05:05,034 --> 00:05:09,850
blocked, and AI-detected passwords are
explicitly excluded from push protection.

73
00:05:10,190 --> 00:05:14,774
A block is a prompt, not a wall,
unless delegated bypass is configured.

74
00:05:15,114 --> 00:05:19,934
And coverage differs by provider, in three
independent columns: whether the provider

75
00:05:19,974 --> 00:05:24,389
is notified for public leaks, whether
push protection blocks the pattern, and

76
00:05:24,429 --> 00:05:26,357
whether there is a validity check.

77
00:05:26,697 --> 00:05:30,142
For an AI and ML engineer
this table is worth a minute.

78
00:05:30,182 --> 00:05:35,402
Keys of OpenAI, Anthropic, Hugging Face
user access tokens, and several other model

79
00:05:35,442 --> 00:05:40,598
providers: yes in all three columns. A
Google API key: provider notified, validity

80
00:05:40,638 --> 00:05:43,386
checked, and not blocked
by push protection.

81
00:05:43,726 --> 00:05:48,696
A Google Gemini API key: not notified,
not blocked, a user alert only.

82
00:05:48,736 --> 00:05:54,064
Mistral, Cohere, DeepSeek, Pinecone:
not notified, but blocked and checked.

83
00:05:54,104 --> 00:05:59,933
Perplexity, LangSmith, Weights and Biases
keys: not notified, blocked, no validity

84
00:05:59,973 --> 00:06:05,516
check. PyPI tokens, npm tokens and GitHub
personal access tokens: notified and

85
00:06:05,556 --> 00:06:06,089
blocked.

86
00:06:06,429 --> 00:06:09,735
The list changes. Re-read it
for the providers you use.

87
00:06:10,075 --> 00:06:13,569
And partner notification
doesn't guarantee revocation.

88
00:06:13,609 --> 00:06:19,000
GitHub revokes its own leaked tokens.
Anthropic documents automatic deactivation of

89
00:06:19,040 --> 00:06:24,170
keys found in public GitHub repositories.
Hugging Face lets anyone invalidate a

90
00:06:24,210 --> 00:06:29,602
leaked token through a revocation endpoint.
But an xAI key flagged on the second of

91
00:06:29,642 --> 00:06:33,398
March 2025 stayed valid until
the thirtieth of April 2025.

92
00:06:33,738 --> 00:06:38,600
Unverified: whether OpenAI disables API
keys it finds on the public internet could

93
00:06:38,640 --> 00:06:43,622
not be confirmed from a primary page, and
how Hugging Face and OpenAI handle partner

94
00:06:43,662 --> 00:06:47,268
notifications from GitHub isn't
documented in the pages read.

95
00:06:47,608 --> 00:06:51,765
Scanners. As of the first of October
2026 the textbook lists five.

96
00:06:51,805 --> 00:06:56,660
TruffleHog, which verifies candidates
against provider APIs and can enumerate

97
00:06:56,700 --> 00:07:01,810
deleted and hidden commits. gitleaks, which
declares itself feature-complete with

98
00:07:01,850 --> 00:07:06,769
security patches only. Betterleaks, to
which gitleaks' author moved, and whose

99
00:07:06,809 --> 00:07:11,728
governance and detection-quality claims
come from one news article and are not

100
00:07:11,768 --> 00:07:16,560
independently verified. detect-secrets,
whose last release is from May 2024.

101
00:07:16,600 --> 00:07:19,485
And git-secrets, which
has no tagged releases.

102
00:07:19,825 --> 00:07:23,253
None is installed for the
course, so none is demonstrated.

103
00:07:23,293 --> 00:07:28,158
The advice: run your choice in two places.
As a pre-commit hook for fast feedback,

104
00:07:28,198 --> 00:07:31,686
and in CI over the full history,
where it can't be skipped.

105
00:07:32,026 --> 00:07:34,624
Dependabot is three
features that share a name.

106
00:07:34,964 --> 00:07:40,044
Alerts tell you that a dependency on the
default branch has a known vulnerability.

107
00:07:40,084 --> 00:07:44,914
A dependency is a package your project
uses. Alerts need the dependency graph.

108
00:07:44,954 --> 00:07:49,722
Only advisories reviewed by GitHub raise
alerts. Archived repositories aren't

109
00:07:49,762 --> 00:07:54,593
scanned. And for Actions, alerts exist
only for actions referenced by semantic

110
00:07:54,633 --> 00:07:56,216
version, not by commit ID.

111
00:07:56,556 --> 00:08:00,993
Security updates open a pull request that
raises a vulnerable dependency to the

112
00:08:01,033 --> 00:08:03,374
minimum patched version. They need alerts.

113
00:08:03,714 --> 00:08:09,478
Version updates open pull requests to keep
dependencies current, vulnerable or not.

114
00:08:09,518 --> 00:08:13,814
They are enabled by committing
a file, .github/dependabot.yml.

115
00:08:13,854 --> 00:08:19,479
Since the fourteenth of July 2026 a default
cooldown of 3 days applies to version

116
00:08:19,519 --> 00:08:21,997
updates and not to security updates.

117
00:08:22,337 --> 00:08:27,983
All three are free on every plan. And note
the trade-off with the advice of video

118
00:08:28,023 --> 00:08:33,881
159: pinned actions get no Dependabot alerts,
so version updates must move the pins.

119
00:08:34,221 --> 00:08:39,419
Two things older configurations get wrong.
The reviewers option was removed on the

120
00:08:39,459 --> 00:08:44,786
eighth of August 2025 in favor of CODEOWNERS,
the file that maps paths to reviewers.

121
00:08:44,826 --> 00:08:50,025
And the comment commands, such as asking
Dependabot to merge in a comment, stopped

122
00:08:50,065 --> 00:08:55,136
working on the twenty-seventh of January
2026. Without explicit configuration at

123
00:08:55,176 --> 00:08:58,777
most five version-update pull
requests stay open at once.

124
00:08:58,817 --> 00:09:01,780
Security updates don't
count toward that limit.

125
00:09:02,120 --> 00:09:06,953
Code scanning analyzes code for vulnerabilities
and coding errors with CodeQL, or

126
00:09:06,993 --> 00:09:11,887
with third-party tools that upload results.
It runs on GitHub Actions and consumes

127
00:09:11,927 --> 00:09:16,700
minutes. It is free on public repositories
and needs GitHub Code Security, at 30

128
00:09:16,740 --> 00:09:20,069
dollars per active committer
per month, on private ones.

129
00:09:20,409 --> 00:09:25,207
GitHub recommends default setup. One
documented surprise: if a repository with

130
00:09:25,247 --> 00:09:29,921
default setup sees no pushes and no pull
requests for six months, the weekly

131
00:09:29,961 --> 00:09:34,945
scheduled scan is disabled. And a boundary:
code scanning doesn't find secrets or

132
00:09:34,985 --> 00:09:38,294
vulnerable dependencies.
Those are the features above.

133
00:09:38,634 --> 00:09:41,888
A security policy and private
vulnerability reporting.

134
00:09:41,928 --> 00:09:46,829
A SECURITY.md file states which versions
you support and how to report a problem.

135
00:09:46,869 --> 00:09:51,038
An organization can provide a default
through its .github repository.

136
00:09:51,078 --> 00:09:56,101
Private vulnerability reporting is a separate
feature that administrators of public

137
00:09:56,141 --> 00:09:57,566
repositories can enable.

138
00:09:57,906 --> 00:10:02,529
It adds a "Report a vulnerability" button
on the Advisories page, and the report

139
00:10:02,569 --> 00:10:07,250
lands in a private draft advisory. The
reason to do both is a case you'll hear in

140
00:10:07,290 --> 00:10:11,971
the next video: a researcher who found a
contractor's leaked credentials couldn't

141
00:10:12,011 --> 00:10:14,769
find a way to report them
and went to the press.

142
00:10:16,609 --> 00:10:20,745
A picture helps. Think of the commits you
haven't pushed as editions that haven't

143
00:10:20,785 --> 00:10:21,673
left the building.

144
00:10:22,013 --> 00:10:26,751
From the last video: publishing a correction
doesn't recall yesterday's edition, and

145
00:10:26,791 --> 00:10:29,709
withdrawing it from your
own archive doesn't either.

146
00:10:29,749 --> 00:10:34,316
The textbook said that analogy breaks for
unpushed commits: an edition that never

147
00:10:34,356 --> 00:10:36,136
left the building can be pulped.

148
00:10:36,476 --> 00:10:41,311
A push-time check is the person at the
loading dock who looks into every box before

149
00:10:41,351 --> 00:10:46,011
it leaves. If one box contains the wrong
edition, the whole shipment is refused.

150
00:10:46,051 --> 00:10:50,827
Putting a correction slip on top of the
shipment doesn't help: the box is still in

151
00:10:50,867 --> 00:10:55,761
it. You have to take the box out. And because
the shipment is still in the building,

152
00:10:55,801 --> 00:10:56,231
you can.

153
00:10:56,571 --> 00:11:01,457
The textbook's sentence: before the first
push is the one moment at which rewriting

154
00:11:01,497 --> 00:11:05,907
history fully removes a secret. A
push-time block exists to keep you there.

155
00:11:06,247 --> 00:11:10,932
Then two things the picture needs. The
checker has a list of what the wrong editions

156
00:11:10,972 --> 00:11:15,431
look like. Anything not on the list goes
through. And there are two checkers who

157
00:11:15,471 --> 00:11:20,043
look alike: the one at your own desk, the
client-side hook, whom you can walk past

158
00:11:20,083 --> 00:11:24,093
with one option, and the one at the
dock, on the server, whom you can't.

159
00:11:24,133 --> 00:11:27,411
Local hooks are advice. A
server-side check is enforcement.

160
00:11:29,251 --> 00:11:33,502
Try it now, thirty seconds, on
paper. Write X for the server's tip.

161
00:11:33,542 --> 00:11:38,753
To its right, draw commit A, which adds
the key, then B, other work, then C, which

162
00:11:38,793 --> 00:11:42,595
deletes the file. Now list the
commits a push would deliver.

163
00:11:42,635 --> 00:11:44,708
Is A among them? Say it out loud.

164
00:11:49,548 --> 00:11:53,177
Yes. A is still delivered, so
the push is declined again.

165
00:11:53,217 --> 00:11:58,390
Row 2 is what people try. Row 3 is the
repair. The check doesn't look at the tip.

166
00:11:58,430 --> 00:12:01,608
It looks at every commit
the push would introduce.

167
00:12:03,448 --> 00:12:05,669
Step 1: the server-side guard.

168
00:12:06,009 --> 00:12:10,744
Read the hook. A pre-receive hook runs
on the receiving side before any ref is

169
00:12:10,784 --> 00:12:14,172
updated, and rejects the whole
push by exiting non-zero.

170
00:12:14,212 --> 00:12:19,131
For every ref in the push it lists the
commits the push would introduce, with git

171
00:12:19,171 --> 00:12:23,355
rev-list of the new tip, excluding
everything the server already has.

172
00:12:23,395 --> 00:12:26,844
For each of those commits it
runs git grep for a pattern.

173
00:12:26,884 --> 00:12:30,089
One match, and it prints the
commit and exits with 1.

174
00:12:30,429 --> 00:12:32,466
Step 2: a push with a secret.

175
00:12:32,806 --> 00:12:35,565
git commit is 🟢 SAFE, and
git push is 🟡 CAUTION.

176
00:12:35,905 --> 00:12:40,897
"Push declined", naming commit 49384d9,
and "remote rejected, pre-receive hook

177
00:12:40,937 --> 00:12:43,348
declined". Nothing reached the server.

178
00:12:43,688 --> 00:12:45,930
Step 3: the natural reaction.

179
00:12:46,270 --> 00:12:51,867
git rm is 🟡: it deletes the file on disk
and removes its index entry, its line in

180
00:12:51,907 --> 00:12:57,296
the staging area. Two unpushed commits
now: one adds the file, one removes it.

181
00:12:57,336 --> 00:13:01,194
The tip is clean. Is the push
accepted? Say it out loud.

182
00:13:06,034 --> 00:13:09,318
Declined again, and it names
the same commit, 49384d9.

183
00:13:09,358 --> 00:13:14,182
The push would deliver that commit, whose
snapshot contains the key, whatever a

184
00:13:14,222 --> 00:13:19,293
later commit does. The textbook adds:
GitHub's push protection behaves the same way

185
00:13:19,333 --> 00:13:23,849
and for the same reason. The secret
has to be removed from the commit that

186
00:13:23,889 --> 00:13:24,711
introduced it.

187
00:13:25,051 --> 00:13:27,567
Step 4: replace the unpushed commits.

188
00:13:27,907 --> 00:13:32,819
git reset --soft to the upstream tip is
🟡 CAUTION: it moves the branch to the

189
00:13:32,859 --> 00:13:36,871
upstream tip, and the index and
working tree keep your changes.

190
00:13:36,911 --> 00:13:40,794
The preview is git log for the
unpushed range, which you saw.

191
00:13:40,834 --> 00:13:43,559
The recovery is git
reset --soft ORIG_HEAD.

192
00:13:43,899 --> 00:13:48,512
Here the index after the reset holds the
net effect of the two commits, which is

193
00:13:48,552 --> 00:13:53,223
nothing: the file was added and removed.
So the status is empty, and the only new

194
00:13:53,263 --> 00:13:54,736
commit is the ignore rule.

195
00:13:55,076 --> 00:13:59,977
Accepted. The server's main has two commits:
the original one and "Ignore .env".

196
00:14:00,017 --> 00:14:02,941
There's the cheap moment
from the opening, used.

197
00:14:03,281 --> 00:14:09,166
Here's the repair as a picture. Before it,
main was two commits ahead of the server:

198
00:14:09,206 --> 00:14:13,468
49384d9, which added the file,
and 8546eb9, which removed it.

199
00:14:13,808 --> 00:14:19,242
After the soft reset and one new commit,
main and the server agree on 5584d4f.

200
00:14:19,282 --> 00:14:24,856
Commit 49384d9 exists only in your local
object database and reflog, Git's local

201
00:14:24,896 --> 00:14:29,628
record of where your branches have
been. It never left the building.

202
00:14:29,968 --> 00:14:32,036
Step 5: the client-side hook.

203
00:14:32,376 --> 00:14:37,239
A pre-commit hook that looks at the staged
additions with git diff --cached and

204
00:14:37,279 --> 00:14:40,529
refuses a commit whose added
lines match the pattern.

205
00:14:40,569 --> 00:14:45,370
It catches the mistake one step earlier:
the commit is refused, exit status 1.

206
00:14:45,710 --> 00:14:50,342
git commit --no-verify is 🟡 CAUTION:
as git commit, without two hooks.

207
00:14:50,382 --> 00:14:54,079
The developer skips the hook.
What stops the secret now?

208
00:14:54,119 --> 00:14:55,481
Make your prediction.

209
00:15:00,321 --> 00:15:04,617
The commit succeeds, 4be6dc6. And
the push is declined by the server.

210
00:15:04,657 --> 00:15:09,897
The comment in the transcript is the rule:
the client hook is advice, and the server

211
00:15:09,937 --> 00:15:11,153
hook is enforcement.

212
00:15:11,493 --> 00:15:15,931
On GitHub the corresponding enforcement
points are push protection, push rulesets

213
00:15:15,971 --> 00:15:17,037
and required checks.

214
00:15:17,377 --> 00:15:21,373
Step 6: when the secret is in
an earlier unpushed commit.

215
00:15:21,413 --> 00:15:27,250
Replay labs/run ch21b/lab-30-1-push-check.
Here there are two unpushed commits, and

216
00:15:27,290 --> 00:15:32,206
the secret is in the first. The second
is real work that must be kept.

217
00:15:32,246 --> 00:15:38,012
A soft reset would mix them. The tool is
an interactive rebase, which replays your

218
00:15:38,052 --> 00:15:40,278
commits and stops where you ask.

219
00:15:40,618 --> 00:15:46,051
git rebase -i is 🟡: it replaces commits with
new ones, and the reflog keeps the old.

220
00:15:46,091 --> 00:15:51,197
The todo list is changed in one word:
pick becomes edit for the commit with the

221
00:15:51,237 --> 00:15:56,474
secret. Git stops there. The file is taken
out of the index with git rm --cached,

222
00:15:56,514 --> 00:16:01,100
which is 🟡 CAUTION, an ignore rule
is added, and the commit is amended.

223
00:16:01,140 --> 00:16:06,377
git commit --amend is 🟡 CAUTION. Then the
rebase continues and replays the second

224
00:16:06,417 --> 00:16:06,833
commit.

225
00:16:10,173 --> 00:16:13,947
Two commits again, with new
IDs, 1668a86 and dc90cbc.

226
00:16:13,987 --> 00:16:19,992
Before pushing, the transcript checks the
same thing the server will check: does any

227
00:16:20,032 --> 00:16:24,957
commit to be pushed contain the key?
None does. The push is accepted.

228
00:16:25,297 --> 00:16:27,532
Step 7: what the check cannot see.

229
00:16:27,872 --> 00:16:32,074
A file with a database password, an
ordinary phrase, in a YAML file.

230
00:16:32,114 --> 00:16:35,817
The pattern list knows the dummy
keys. Is the push accepted?

231
00:16:35,857 --> 00:16:36,815
Say it out loud.

232
00:16:41,655 --> 00:16:46,332
Accepted, exit status 0. A secret whose
shape isn't in the pattern list goes

233
00:16:46,372 --> 00:16:51,545
straight through. That's the second point
of "what it does not cover", made visible.

234
00:16:51,885 --> 00:17:00,391
Step 8: a Dependabot configuration. Replay
labs/run ch21b/lab-30-2-dependabot.

235
00:17:03,731 --> 00:17:08,354
Three entries. The uv ecosystem for the
Python project, weekly, with a group that

236
00:17:08,394 --> 00:17:11,521
collects minor and patch
updates into one pull request.

237
00:17:11,561 --> 00:17:16,300
docker, weekly. And github-actions, weekly,
with a cooldown of seven days, which is

238
00:17:16,340 --> 00:17:20,734
longer than the default of three. The
file was assembled from the keys in the

239
00:17:20,774 --> 00:17:24,419
options reference, parse-checked
locally, and not run on GitHub.

240
00:17:24,759 --> 00:17:29,618
A small check script: the version must be
2, and each entry needs an ecosystem, a

241
00:17:29,658 --> 00:17:32,944
directory and a schedule.
Three lines, nothing missing.

242
00:17:32,984 --> 00:17:37,904
It proves that the file is well formed,
and nothing about what GitHub will do with

243
00:17:37,944 --> 00:17:38,085
it.

244
00:17:38,425 --> 00:17:42,853
Part B of Lab 30.1, on your practice
repository, in your normal shell.

245
00:17:42,893 --> 00:17:48,149
The interface changes. The lab text and the
linked documentation are the reference,

246
00:17:48,189 --> 00:17:53,383
and no GitHub output was captured by the
authors. Use only the dummy value the lab

247
00:17:53,423 --> 00:17:57,212
prescribes. Never a real
credential, not even a revoked one.

248
00:17:57,552 --> 00:18:02,701
gh repo edit is 🟡 CAUTION: it changes
settings. The preview is gh repo view with

249
00:18:02,741 --> 00:18:06,528
JSON fields, and the recovery
is to set the previous value.

250
00:18:06,568 --> 00:18:11,458
The second command reads the setting
back. The lab notes that it needs admin

251
00:18:11,498 --> 00:18:13,339
permission on the repository.

252
00:18:13,679 --> 00:18:16,641
Then the lab has you push
the dummy file on a branch.

253
00:18:16,681 --> 00:18:19,587
Before you push, predict:
will this push be blocked?

254
00:18:19,627 --> 00:18:24,346
Think about what the chapter said about the
dummy values: they're spelled so that no

255
00:18:24,386 --> 00:18:28,765
scanner pattern matches. Then watch what
happens, and find in the repository's

256
00:18:28,805 --> 00:18:32,051
security settings where the
two features show their state.

257
00:18:32,091 --> 00:18:34,657
Delete the branch
afterwards, as the lab says.

258
00:18:36,497 --> 00:18:38,145
Five mistakes to watch for.

259
00:18:38,485 --> 00:18:42,942
Deleting the file in a new commit after a
block. Root cause: the push would still

260
00:18:42,982 --> 00:18:47,385
deliver the commit that introduced the
secret; the secret has to be removed from

261
00:18:47,425 --> 00:18:48,051
that commit.

262
00:18:48,391 --> 00:18:53,268
Bypassing the block with "I'll fix it later".
Root cause: after the push the secret

263
00:18:53,308 --> 00:18:57,355
is in every repository that fetches
it, and the cheap moment is over.

264
00:18:57,695 --> 00:19:00,918
Assuming push protection
covers a private repository.

265
00:19:00,958 --> 00:19:05,659
Root cause: user push protection guards
only pushes to public repositories; a

266
00:19:05,699 --> 00:19:10,277
private repository without Secret
Protection has no push-time check at all.

267
00:19:10,617 --> 00:19:15,225
Assuming every kind of secret is blocked.
Root cause: push protection blocks

268
00:19:15,265 --> 00:19:20,301
high-confidence provider patterns; generic
passwords and connection strings are not

269
00:19:20,341 --> 00:19:22,869
blocked, and coverage differs by provider.

270
00:19:23,209 --> 00:19:27,805
Treating a pre-commit hook as the
control. Root cause: it is skipped with

271
00:19:27,845 --> 00:19:32,315
--no-verify and is not cloned; only
a server-side check is enforcement.

272
00:19:34,155 --> 00:19:38,969
Now, out of the lab. An AI team starts a
new private repository for a retrieval

273
00:19:39,009 --> 00:19:44,069
service. On day one the tech lead writes
down the baseline, and for each layer what

274
00:19:44,109 --> 00:19:44,684
it misses.

275
00:19:45,024 --> 00:19:49,449
Push protection for the repository, with
Secret Protection, because user push

276
00:19:49,489 --> 00:19:52,580
protection wouldn't cover a
private repository at all.

277
00:19:52,620 --> 00:19:56,871
What it misses: generic passwords, and
providers outside the pattern list.

278
00:19:56,911 --> 00:20:01,452
The team checks the supported-pattern
table for the three providers it uses and

279
00:20:01,492 --> 00:20:03,597
finds that one of them isn't blocked.

280
00:20:03,937 --> 00:20:08,677
A scanner as a pre-commit hook and
again in CI over the full history.

281
00:20:08,717 --> 00:20:12,695
What it misses at the desk:
anyone who passes --no-verify.

282
00:20:12,735 --> 00:20:14,774
That's why it also runs in CI.

283
00:20:15,114 --> 00:20:20,154
A Dependabot file with three ecosystems,
and CODEOWNERS for review, since the old

284
00:20:20,194 --> 00:20:25,234
reviewers option is gone. What it misses:
alerts for actions pinned by commit ID,

285
00:20:25,274 --> 00:20:28,810
which is why the Actions entry
is there to move the pins.

286
00:20:29,150 --> 00:20:33,234
Code scanning with default setup. What
it misses: secrets and vulnerable

287
00:20:33,274 --> 00:20:35,869
dependencies, which are
the other layers' job.

288
00:20:36,209 --> 00:20:40,520
And a SECURITY.md with a contact.
What it misses: nothing technical.

289
00:20:40,560 --> 00:20:45,767
It exists so that the person who finds the
team's mistake can tell the team first.

290
00:20:47,607 --> 00:20:53,944
Your turn. Do Lab 30.1, "Push protection,
and what a push-time check catches", in

291
00:20:53,984 --> 00:21:00,556
lab-manual/m30-repository-security.md. Part
A runs in labs/shell, and Part B in your

292
00:21:00,596 --> 00:21:01,580
normal shell.

293
00:21:01,920 --> 00:21:06,833
In Part A, before each push, predict whether
the server will accept it and, if not,

294
00:21:06,873 --> 00:21:11,727
which commit it will name. Before the
repair, decide which tool fits: a soft reset

295
00:21:11,767 --> 00:21:16,621
or an interactive rebase, and why. In Part
B, predict whether the dummy value will

296
00:21:16,661 --> 00:21:17,277
be blocked.

297
00:21:17,617 --> 00:21:24,220
The challenge is Exercise 30.6, "A
baseline for a new ML repository", in

298
00:21:24,260 --> 00:21:27,726
exercises/m26-m31-actions-security.md.

299
00:21:29,566 --> 00:21:32,782
Read the question on screen.
Say your answer out loud.

300
00:21:37,622 --> 00:21:42,225
A strong answer starts by saying exactly
what "for users" means: whose pushes, to

301
00:21:42,265 --> 00:21:46,984
which repositories. Then it gives three
leaks that are different in kind, each tied

302
00:21:47,024 --> 00:21:51,513
to a documented limit: one about where
the push goes, one about what the secret

303
00:21:51,553 --> 00:21:54,781
looks like, one about what a
person can do at the prompt.

304
00:21:55,121 --> 00:21:59,212
It may add a fourth about secrets that
never pass through a push at all.

305
00:21:59,252 --> 00:22:03,975
The follow-up is the hook of this video: a
developer hits a block, deletes the file

306
00:22:04,015 --> 00:22:08,394
in a new commit and pushes again. Say
what happens, why, and what the correct

307
00:22:08,434 --> 00:22:11,320
recovery is, with the two
tools and when each fits.

308
00:22:13,160 --> 00:22:14,088
Let's land this.

309
00:22:14,428 --> 00:22:16,087
You should now be able to say:

310
00:22:16,427 --> 00:22:21,455
Secret scanning reads the whole history and
more; push protection blocks recognized

311
00:22:21,495 --> 00:22:26,462
patterns at push time, by default only for
a user's pushes to public repositories.

312
00:22:26,802 --> 00:22:31,773
It does not block generic secrets, coverage
differs by provider, and a block is a

313
00:22:31,813 --> 00:22:34,557
prompt unless delegated
bypass is configured.

314
00:22:34,897 --> 00:22:39,543
A deletion commit does not unblock a
push, because the push still delivers the

315
00:22:39,583 --> 00:22:44,110
commit with the secret; replace the
unpushed commits with a soft reset or an

316
00:22:44,150 --> 00:22:45,251
interactive rebase.

317
00:22:45,591 --> 00:22:49,207
A client-side hook is advice; a
server-side check is enforcement.

318
00:22:49,547 --> 00:22:54,371
Dependabot is alerts, security updates
and version updates; code scanning finds

319
00:22:54,411 --> 00:22:59,542
neither secrets nor vulnerable dependencies;
a security policy gives reporters a way

320
00:22:59,582 --> 00:23:00,342
to reach you.

321
00:23:02,182 --> 00:23:09,140
Read sections 21B.12 and 21B.13 of
Chapter 21B. Do Lab 30.2, "A Dependabot

322
00:23:09,180 --> 00:23:14,531
configuration", in
lab-manual/m30-repository-security.md.

323
00:23:14,571 --> 00:23:22,003
Then do Exercise 30.3, "Blocked or not?",
and Exercise 30.4, "The baseline of a

324
00:23:22,043 --> 00:23:27,772
public repository", in
exercises/m26-m31-actions-security.md.

325
00:23:28,112 --> 00:23:33,143
Today you unblocked a push the right way,
twice, and you can say what a push-time

326
00:23:33,183 --> 00:23:37,088
check never sees. Do the repair
once yourself in the lab shell.

327
00:23:37,128 --> 00:23:41,283
Next time: responding to a leaked
secret, six steps, in this order.

328
00:23:41,323 --> 00:23:44,539
Until then, look at the
state first and type second.

329
00:23:44,579 --> 00:23:46,042
See you in the next one.
